# Ownership for the files that decide what CI proves and what the project ships.
#
# Read this as a marker, not as an active control. Two things currently make it
# inert, and both are worth knowing before anyone relies on it:
#
#   * CODEOWNERS only does anything once branch protection requires review from
#     code owners, which needs a public repository or a paid plan.
#   * With a single maintainer it cannot bite even then. GitHub will not accept
#     a pull request author's own approval, so every human pull request here
#     would be permanently unapprovable, and clearing it by admin bypass voids
#     the control for bot pull requests at the same time.
#
# It is kept because the day a second maintainer appears, these are the paths
# where a second pair of eyes matters most, and reconstructing that list later
# is harder than writing it down now.
#
# The security argument for these paths specifically: a pull request runs the
# workflow files *as they exist in that pull request*. A change that deletes the
# zizmor job, or the licence and advisory gate, disables the very check that
# would have objected to it. Requiring an owner here is what closes that loop.
#
# No `*` catch-all: with one owner it assigns every path to the same person and
# changes nothing, while making the specific entries below look like they carry
# weight they do not.

# CI definitions, the composite action, and the changed-path classifier that
# decides which jobs run at all.
/.github/               @MarcusKainth
/scripts/               @MarcusKainth

# Supply-chain surface: the licence/advisory/bans policy, the resolved graph
# every gate is evaluated against, and the generated attribution inventory.
/deny.toml              @MarcusKainth
/about.toml             @MarcusKainth
/Cargo.lock             @MarcusKainth
/THIRD-PARTY.md         @MarcusKainth

# Published security claims. These have been wrong before — the nightly they
# described was disabled for eighteen days — so a change here should be a
# deliberate one.
/SECURITY.md            @MarcusKainth
